out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Two readings of one opinion. Asked which reader was right, Mrs Khoury said neither, and that they were wrong in opposite directions: one had read a limited finding as a settled one and the other had read a limit as a retreat. Mr Shihabi said the lawyer was not unreasonable, since the second sentence does take the first one back, and Mr Zahreddine said it does not take it back and he chose every word of it — what he found was that the slab was poured with the roller out of service, and what he did not find was when the roller went out, and those are two different sentences and the second is not an apology for the first. Mrs Tarabishi said the lawyer had simply misread him, and Mrs Oueidat said he had misread it because of where it sits: the limit is in a sentence of its own after the finding, and anything in its own sentence after a finding is read as a second thought. Four units of this course have said that, and here is the invoice. Dr Qabbani said we should go back and ask him to rewrite it, and Mrs Khoury said we read him his own second sentence and ask him one question — what would have to arrive for the date to be established — and he will say the service log, because it is already in his sentence. Mr Zahreddine said that then he has written it himself, which is the whole point of the move. Mrs Rizk said that somebody has to tell the claims handler he cannot close the file, and Mrs Khoury said not tell but ask, and that this is what is new in this unit: in 1.7 and in 3.7 the reader had nothing to write and nothing to do, so we left the reader alone, and here the reader is going to decline a claim on Thursday. Mr Tayara asked what the question to the handler is, and it is which of the two things the engineer says he has not seen do you hold — nine words, and the answer is the service log, in a filing cabinet forty metres from his desk. Mrs Ajouz asked how long nobody had asked that, and Mr Zahreddine said eleven weeks: two engineers, one tribunal date and about nine hundred thousand pounds in fees, and the document was theirs throughout. Nobody read the limit as an instruction, which is what a limit is. Mr Shihabi asked whether a limit is an instruction, and Mrs Oueidat said to the reader, yes — I have not seen the service log tells a reader what to go and find, and it is the most useful sentence in the document, and the one both treated as throat-clearing. Mr Attar asked whether we correct the lawyer, and Mrs Khoury said only if he asks, as in 3.7, but that he will ask here because he is about to spend money: a reader who has to act will come back to you, and a reader who only has an opinion will not. And Mrs Ajouz said the thing she will take away is that the two of them stopped at the same place again — one closed the file and one opened a second instruction, and neither of them opened the cabinet.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The number to write on the board is eleven weeks, two engineers and a filing cabinet forty metres away.** **Then ask the room the question the cycle turns on: why did nobody ask?** **The answer is in Mrs Oueidat\'s line** — **both readers treated _I have not seen the service log_ as throat-clearing, because that is what a qualification sounds like.** **A limit is an instruction to the reader and it is the only sentence in a professional document that tells its reader what to go and find.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** Telling the lawyer he has misread it. → **✓** say what is correct first.   **✗** Rewriting the engineer\'s sentence for him. → **✓** read it to him and ask.   **✗** Leaving the handler alone. → **✓** he declines the claim on Thursday.   **✗** _Which of these do you hold?_ with the stress on _you_. → **✓** stress _hold_.   **✗** Treating _cannot be established_ and _I have not seen_ as the same. → **✓** two different conversations.'));
